
<% content_for :title, "Boredom & Restlessness Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-boredom">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / BOREDOM &amp; RESTLESSNESS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BORED OUT</span>
        <span>OF YOUR</span>
        <span>GODDAMN SKULL?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR EMPTY HOURS &amp; RESTLESS ENERGY.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE EMPTY SPACE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / THE EMPTY SPACE
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE YOU<br>
        LOOKING FOR?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes sobriety is boring. There's no dramatic
          revelation hiding underneath it. You have hours
          to fill, nothing sounds particularly interesting,
          and drinking used to be an easy way to make
          an ordinary evening feel different.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol didn't just help you escape unpleasant
          feelings. Sometimes it was fun. It made things
          more entertaining, changed your mood, and gave
          you something to look forward to. You don't
          need to pretend you hated drinking to
          understand why you stopped.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now there's a space where drinking used to be.
          You might miss the anticipation, the excitement,
          the ritual, or the way alcohol made doing
          absolutely nothing feel like an activity.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before deciding you need a drink, figure out
          what you're actually missing. Are you looking
          for stimulation, relaxation, company,
          creativity, novelty, or just something
          to break up the day?
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Boredom is uncomfortable. It isn't an
            emergency, and it doesn't require alcohol.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YOUR PATTERNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR PATTERNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHEN DOES<br>
        IT HIT?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR VULNERABLE HOURS</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention to evenings, weekends,
            unstructured afternoons, and times
            when you have nothing planned.
            These are opportunities for boredom
            to turn into bargaining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Being alone can make the hours feel
            longer. So can being stuck inside
            because of pain or limited mobility.
            When you can't do the activities
            you normally enjoy, drinking may
            start looking like an easy substitute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when your thoughts shift from
            "I'm bored" to "I could have a drink."
            That transition matters. It's the point
            where an ordinary feeling becomes
            a potential relapse risk.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU ACTUALLY WANT</h3>

          <p>
            Not every restless feeling means
            you need more stimulation.
            Sometimes you're tired, lonely,
            uncomfortable, or frustrated
            with your circumstances.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask yourself what would actually
            improve the next hour.
            Do you want something interesting
            to think about? Something creative
            to work on? A conversation?
            A change of scenery?
            Or permission to rest?
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider your mood, too.
            If restlessness comes with less
            sleep, racing thoughts, unusually
            high energy, or a sudden need
            to start projects and spend money,
            don't assume it's ordinary boredom.
            Those changes deserve attention
            from your treatment team.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR ACTUAL OPTIONS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR ACTUAL OPTIONS
      </p>

      <h2>
        SOMETHING<br>
        YOU'LL REALLY DO.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>FIVE MINUTES</h3>

          <p>
            You don't need to plan your entire
            evening. Start with five minutes
            of something that changes
            what you're paying attention to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put on a favorite song.
            Make tea or a snack.
            Look through ideas for your next
            glass-engraving project.
            Step outside for fresh air
            if you're able, or spend
            a few minutes with Banjo.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose something small enough
            that you won't talk yourself
            out of starting it.
            Five minutes isn't supposed
            to transform your life.
            It's supposed to interrupt
            the automatic routine.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>A WHOLE EVENING</h3>

          <p>
            Give yourself something to get
            involved in. Work on a glass-engraving
            design, experiment with a new
            craft, draw, paint, or plan
            something creative.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch a movie you've been meaning
            to see, play a game, work on your
            website, read, or explore
            a subject that genuinely
            interests you.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have to turn every
            sober evening into a productive
            accomplishment. Entertainment
            is a perfectly legitimate
            way to spend your time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If nothing sounds interesting,
            choose one thing and give it
            fifteen minutes before deciding
            whether to switch.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>LOW ENERGY OR PAIN DAY</h3>

          <p>
            When your back hurts, your energy
            is low, or moving around is difficult,
            don't build a plan that depends
            on physical activity you
            can't comfortably manage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Listen to music or an audiobook.
            Watch something entertaining.
            Sketch an idea, organize
            reference images, or work
            on a small project in a
            comfortable position.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend time with Banjo, talk
            to a friend, or simply rest
            without expecting yourself
            to accomplish something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pain can make alcohol seem like
            a shortcut to feeling different.
            Follow your established pain
            treatment plan rather than
            trying to manage it with drinking.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>SOMETHING WITH SOMEONE</h3>

          <p>
            If being alone is making
            the boredom worse, reach out
            instead of waiting for
            someone to contact you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Call a friend, arrange a visit,
            have someone over, play an
            online game, or find a
            meeting.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need a serious
            conversation about sobriety.
            Sometimes you just want
            to laugh, complain about
            something ridiculous,
            or hear about someone
            else's day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose company that supports
            your sobriety.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / WHEN IT BECOMES A DRINKING URGE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / WHEN IT BECOMES A DRINKING URGE
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOUR NEXT<br>
        TEN MINUTES.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          If boredom turns into wanting alcohol,
          stop treating it like a harmless
          passing thought. Recognize that
          you're considering drinking,
          and decide what you're going
          to do about it.
        </p>

        <p>
          First, create distance from the
          opportunity. Don't go to the
          liquor store, browse alcohol
          delivery, or start making
          plans that involve drinking.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next, play the tape forward.
          Remember what you liked about
          drinking, but don't stop there.
          Think about what happened when
          the buzz wore off, when one
          drink became several, and when
          the consequences started
          adding up.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give yourself ten minutes before
          making any decision. Get something
          to eat or drink that isn't alcohol.
          Change what you're doing.
          Put on a movie, pick up a project,
          contact a friend, or move
          somewhere you feel more settled.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the urge continues, tell someone.
          Contact a trusted friend,
          someone from Harmony, or your therapist. 
          If they aren't available,
          try another person or a
          recovery meeting.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to solve the
          boredom tonight. You don't
          have to suddenly love being
          sober or find a meaningful
          purpose for every empty hour.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You can be bored, restless,
            frustrated, and completely
            unimpressed with your evening.
            You can still stay sober.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
